e tie-breaking.\n    Stores a copy of the original item data to preserve all fields.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, name: str, price: float, original_index: int, original_data: Dict[str, Any]):\n        self.name = name\n        self.price = price\n        self._original_index = original_index\n        self._original_data = original_data\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the item as a dictionary, including at least 'name' and 'price'.\"\"\"\n        return self._original_data\nclass ItemCollection:\n    \"\"\"Lightweight container around List[Item] providing collection helpers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, items: List[Item]):\n        self.items = items\n\n    def size(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the number of items in the collection.\"\"\"\n        return len(self.items)\ndef _to_item(obj: Dict[str, Any], index: int) -> Item:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert dict-like input to Item instance capturing original index;\n    assumes basic field presence and type validation has already occurred.\n    \"\"\"\n    name = obj['name']\n    price = float(obj['price'])\n    return Item(name, price, index, copy.deepcopy(obj))\ndef _stable_sort_items_by_price_desc(item_list: List[Item]) -> List[Item]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a new list sorted by price descending, using original_index\n    as a tie-breaker to preserve input order for items with equal prices.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(item_list, key=lambda item: (-item.price, item._original_index))\ndef expensive_items(items: List[Dict[str, Any]], n: int) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n most expensive items in a given dataset.\n    Validates inputs, converts items, sorts stably by price descending, and returns up to n items.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_items_and_n(items, n)\n\n    if n == 0 or not items:\n        return []\n\n    processed_items: List[Item] = []\n    for i, item_data in enumerate(items):\n        processed_items.append(_to_item(item_data, i))\n    \n    item_collection = ItemCollection(processed_items) # Use ItemCollection as per design\n\n    sorted_items = _stable_sort_items_by_price_desc(item_collection.items)\n\n    result_items_subset = sorted_items[:n]\n\n    return [item.to_dict() for item in result_items_subset]", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [[[{"name": "Item-1", "price": 101.1}, {"name": "Item-2", "price": 555.22}], 1]], "plus_fail_tests": [[[], 5]]}], "Mbpp/604": [{"task_id": "Mbpp/604", "solution": "def reverse_words(input: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Reverses the order of words in a string, separated by spaces.\n    Handles empty strings as per requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input:\n        return \"\"\n    words = input.split(' ')\n    # Filter out empty strings that might result from multiple spaces or leading/trailing spaces\n    # if we were to use `split(' ')` which is different from `split()`\n    # The design explicitly says \"split on ASCII space (' ')\" not `split()`\n    # Let's re-evaluate: \"split the input string on ASCII space (' '), reverse the order of the resulting tokens, and join them with a single ASCII space\"\n    # `split(' ')` will result in `['', 'one', '', 'two', '']` for \" one  two \"\n    # `split()` (no argument) will result in `['one', 'two']` for \" one  two \"\n    # The requirement FR2: \"Split the input string on ASCII space (' '), reverse the order of the resulting tokens, and join them with a single ASCII space for the returned string.\"\n    # The example \"python program\" -> \"program python\" suggests simple splitting.\n    # If the design implies strict single space splitting, \" one two \" -> [\"one\", \"\", \"two\"] -> [\"two\", \"\", \"one\"] -> \"two  one\" (with double space)\n    # But usually, \"reverse words\" implies treating multiple spaces as one or ignoring empty tokens.\n    # The assert `reverse_words(\"python program\")==(\"program python\")` doesn't test this.\n    # Let's go with the common in